# Beat 10 + 11 (merged) of grpconf/synopsis.md — 5:00 + 1:30.
#
# Payload: grpconf/stage/boblog, 20 198 bytes.
# Database: grpconf/stage/googleapis.desc — 7 771 files, 58 777 types.
#
# Invocation:
#   protolens --descriptor-set $GOOGLEAPIS -I $SRC \
#       --script grpconf/beats/log-full.script $BOBLOG
#
# Synopsis open question 3 recommends merging sessions 3 and 4, because
# the overrides set in beat 10 must still be in force for the export in
# beat 11.  This file implements the merged session.  If they are ever
# split, move the last two steps into a separate export.script and update
# presentation.sh accordingly.
#
# Three moves in order (synopsis beat 10):
#   1. The opaque bytes have names now  (Places payloads resolve)
#   2. The tie — where the tool stops and defers  (Routes v1 vs v2)
#   3. The reconstructed source earns its keep  (v, then w + 4 anomalies)
# Then beat 11: export to prototext, round-trip proof in the shell.
#
# node: search strings — see infer.script's header.  Under googleapis.desc
# the envelope (bobapp.v1.Log / bobapp.v1.Entry) has NO TYPE — Google has
# never heard of Bob's app — so the log opens raw and fields are bare
# numbers.  After each path:field override, the named fields are reachable
# by their schema name.
#
# The three override:  directives below PREFILL but do NOT execute.
# The presenter presses Enter for each.  Announce each one.
#
# Re-pin:
#   protolens --descriptor-set $GOOGLEAPIS -I $SRC \
#       --script grpconf/beats/log-full.script $BOBLOG script \
#     | grep 'error:'

title: The full corpus, and bytes that are messages

steps:
  # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  # OPEN: what was lost
  # -----------------------------------------------------------------------

  - text: |
      Same file.  Bigger dictionary: 7 771 files, 58 777 types, 25.6 MB,
      indexed once.  It still opens in about 50 ms.

      Open by saying what was lost.  In beat 9 the entries could be
      named bobapp.v1.Entry.  Here they cannot: Google has never heard
      of Bob's app.  Neither schema reads the whole file, and no third
      schema is fetched to make the problem go away.

      Press space, then -> to step.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/]
    node: /

  # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  # MOVE 1: the opaque bytes have names now
  # -----------------------------------------------------------------------

  - text: |
      The same entry that was opaque bytes under the small dictionary.

      Under googleapis, the best candidate jumped from -37 to +12.
      Same bytes, same tool, one command apart: junk under one database
      and named outright under the other.

      The command line below targets the first entry's request field
      at its positional path.  Press Enter to apply it.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/]
    node: "1 {"
    override: override /1:4 --as google.maps.places.v1.SearchTextRequest

  - text: |
      A flat blob becomes a tree.

      text_query: "coffee in Grenoble", a location_bias.circle around
      a lat/lng.  Bob's app does more than he thought.  It does not
      just route; it geocodes, and it sends a place name and a
      coordinate to a second Google service on every run.

      Unfold the second entry's request the same way — or press -> to
      let the script do it.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/1]
    node: text_query
    override: override /2:4 --as google.maps.places.v1.SearchTextRequest

  - text: |
      Both Places requests are now readable.  The responses came with
      them, at +45.

      Before we accept a type for a subnode, v on that field opens its
      definition in the .proto reproto wrote in beat 6.  Navigate to
      the request field now, then press v.

      Judging whether a type really fits is not something a score can
      do for you.  What you want in front of you is the schema, in your
      own editor.  Leave with :q.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/1]
    node: text_query

  # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  # MOVE 2: the tie — where the tool stops and defers
  # -----------------------------------------------------------------------

  - text: |
      Now the route request — 84 bytes, the same payload as beat 8.

      Under the small dictionary it was unambiguous: ComputeRoutesRequest
      at -16, thirty-nine points of daylight.

      Under googleapis the picture changed.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/]
    node: "3 {"

  - text: |
      Two candidates.  The same score.

          google.maps.routes.v1.ComputeRoutesRequest    -16
          google.maps.routing.v2.ComputeRoutesRequest   -16

      They render this payload identically, field for field.  v1's field
      numbers are a strict subset of v2's, so v1 can never outrank v2;
      a tie is the ceiling.  The tool is not confused.  Those two
      schemas genuinely say the same thing about these bytes.

      What separates them is not in the payload at all.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/]
    node: "3 {"

  - text: |
      It is two lines up.  An unnamed field, in a message nothing could
      identify, holding the answer in plain text:

          2: "/google.maps.routing.v2.Routes/ComputeRoutes"

      The tie is broken by the part of the file that no schema described.
      Read it out loud.  Pick v2 by hand.  That is the point.
      It ranks.  Then it defers to you.

      Press Enter to commit the choice.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/]
    node: "ComputeRoutes"
    override: override /3:4 --as google.maps.routing.v2.ComputeRoutesRequest

  # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  # MOVE 3: reconstructed source earns its keep, then w + four anomalies
  # -----------------------------------------------------------------------

  - text: |
      Now the part this talk is actually about.

      Navigate to the route request's request field and press w.
      The bytes that each line was decoded from appear underneath it,
      in three severity tiers.

      This is where "non-canonical" stops being an abstraction.
      It is a color on a byte.

      Four anomalies follow.  The display takes 200 ms.  Nobody absorbs
      it in 200 ms.  Go slowly.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/3]
    node: request

  - text: |
      1.  A singular field that occurs twice.

      Perfectly legal on the wire.  The specification says last one wins.
      protoc --decode shows you the second value, note: "ok".

      Both are here, in wire order, because both are on the wire.
      The first one is Bob's API key.

      This program writes his credential into its own log file, in a
      place a normal decoder will never print.
    node: note
    wire-lines:
      from: note
      to: note

  - text: |
      2.  A non-minimal varint.

      A varint may carry trailing padding.  This one decodes to the
      same number it would without the padding, and re-encodes to
      different bytes.  Nothing downstream notices — no parser errors,
      no field changes value, and the difference survives every hop.

      Look at the tail of the byte run: that is the padding.  Beat 4
      noticed it in passing; this is the payoff.
    node: val_ohb
    wire-line: val_ohb

  - text: |
      3.  A field the recovered schema does not declare.

      A newer producer against an older schema.  Nothing is lost: the
      wire type is in the tag, so the shape is readable even though the
      name is not.  It is named here by what it is rather than by what
      it is called.

      This is the one an ordinary decoder drops on the floor.
    node: "99:"
    wire-line: "99:"

  - text: |
      4.  A truncated length-delimited record, at the tail.

      The app was killed mid-write.  The length prefix said more bytes
      than arrived.

      This is the input protoc refuses outright.  The damage is bounded
      to one entry: everything above it read cleanly, and still does.
      The length prefix is what makes the boundary knowable.

      That is all four.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/]
    node: "TRUNCATED_BYTES"

  # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  # BEAT 11: What Alice sends back (merged from export.script)
  # -----------------------------------------------------------------------

  - text: |
      One question left.  We have read the payload, named its fields,
      and pointed at four things standard tooling does not show.

      Did we change any of it?

      Press Enter to write the document out as plain text — an ordinary
      annotated file that Bob can open in any editor with nothing
      installed.  That is what Alice sends back.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/]
    node: /
    override: export $STAGE/boblog.prototext

  - text: |
      The round trip is one command in the shell:

          prototext encode boblog.prototext -o roundtrip.pb
          cmp boblog roundtrip.pb && echo identical

      The leaked key survived.  It would not have survived
      protoc --decode.

      All three advertised promises close in one sentence.

      :quit, and we close on the shell.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/]
    node: /
